B.a3L=s(["Understand what a Python program is","Use the print() function"],t.s)
B.a0U=s(["Print the text: Hello, World!"],t.s)
B.a44=s(["Text must be in quotes: print('Hello, World!')"],t.s)
B.ZF=new A.cy("Think of Python as a super-fast translator. It takes the simple instructions you write and turns them into something a computer can follow instantly.",null)
B.ZJ=new A.cy("Every single line matters. Python reads top-to-bottom, one at a time \u2014 just like you're reading this. It finishes line one before it even glances at line two.",null)
B.Zs=new A.cy("To make Python say something out loud, use `print()` and put your text inside quote marks. The quotes are how Python knows the words are for it to say, not instructions to follow.",'print("Hello, World!")')
B.ZS=new A.cy("That's it. One line, one job. In the next step you'll write it yourself \u2014 and it will be the first program you've ever run.",null)
B.a3r=s([B.ZF,B.ZJ,B.Zs,B.ZS],t.xu)
B.a__=new A.kj(B.a3r)
B.Uj=new A.dm("ex-hello-world","lesson-1","Hello, World!","Use print() to display a friendly greeting. This is the traditional first program in every language.","# Write your code below\n",B.a3L,B.a0U,B.a44,1,B.M,null,B.a__)
B.a3m=s(["Store values in variables","Combine text with variables"],t.s)
B.a22=s(["Create a variable named name","Print Hello followed by the name"],t.s)
B.a0m=s(['A variable uses the = sign: name = "Kofi"'],t.s)
B.Zt=new A.cy("A variable is a labelled box. You put something inside, give the box a name, and later you just say the name instead of repeating what was inside.",null)
B.Zp=new A.cy('You fill a box with `=`. Whatever sits to the right gets stored under the name on the left. Python reads it right-to-left: it figures out `"Kofi"` first, then stores it as `name`.','name = "Kofi"')
B.ZN=new A.cy("Now the name works anywhere you'd use the value. One change to the box, and every place you mention it changes too.","name = 'Ama'\nprint('Hello', name)")
B.Zz=new A.cy("Good variable names describe what's inside. `name` beats `x` \u2014 including for you, a week from now.",null)
B.a2h=s([B.Zt,B.Zp,B.ZN,B.Zz],t.xu)
B.ZV=new A.kj(B.a2h)
B.Ul=new A.dm("ex-variables","lesson-2","Variables and Names","Create a variable called name with your name, then print a greeting using it.","name = \nprint('Hello', name)\n",B.a3m,B.a22,B.a0m,1,B.M,null,B.ZV)
B.a4f=s(["Use +, -, * and /","Understand operator precedence"],t.s)
B.a45=s(["Print the sum of 7 and 5","Print the product of 7 and 5"],t.s)
B.a1M=s(["print(a + b)","Multiplication uses the * symbol"],t.s)
B.Zq=new A.cy("Python does sums the way you'd write them. `+` adds, `-` takes away, `*` multiplies and `/` divides.","print(7 + 5)\nprint(7 - 5)\nprint(7 * 5)\nprint(7 / 5)")
B.ZA=new A.cy("Watch the division though. `/` always gives a decimal, even when the answer is a whole number \u2014 `7 / 5` is `1.4`, not `1`.",null)
B.ZB=new A.cy("Order matters, and Python follows the rules you learned in maths: multiply before you add. So `2 + 3 * 4` is `14`, not `20`. Use brackets when you want to change that.","print(2 + 3 * 4)\nprint((2 + 3) * 4)")
B.ZL=new A.cy("Variables work here too. Store your numbers first, then do the sum \u2014 that way changing a number later means changing it once.",null)
B.a0M=s([B.Zq,B.ZA,B.ZB,B.ZL],t.xu)
B.a_0=new A.kj(B.a0M)
B.Up=new A.dm("ex-arithmetic","lesson-2","Arithmetic","Calculate and print the sum and product of two numbers.","a = 7\nb = 5\n",B.a4f,B.a45,B.a1M,1,B.M,null,B.a_0)
B.a1R=s(["Use if / else","Compare values"],t.s)
B.a4m=s(["Use an if statement","Handle both outcomes"],t.s)
B.a0Z=s(["if score >= 50:","Remember the colon at the end of the condition"],t.s)
B.Zx=new A.cy("So far your programs run the same lines every time. `if` lets them choose \u2014 like a fork in the road.",null)
B.ZK=new A.cy('An `if` asks a yes-or-no question. If the answer is yes, Python runs the indented lines underneath. If not, it skips them. The colon at the end says "the indented part belongs to me".','score = 72\nif score >= 50:\n    print("Pass")')
B.ZQ=new A.cy('`>=` means "greater than or equal to". That little `=` is easy to forget, and forgetting it means a score of exactly 50 fails.',null)
B.ZE=new A.cy("But what about the other answer? `else` is the second fork: Python takes the `if` branch when the question is true, and the `else` branch when it isn't.",'if score >= 50:\n    print("Pass")\nelse:\n    print("Try again")')
B.a1y=s([B.Zx,B.ZK,B.ZQ,B.ZE],t.xu)
B.ZX=new A.kj(B.a1y)
B.Uo=new A.dm("ex-conditions","lesson-3","Making Decisions",'Given a score, print "Pass" when it is 50 or more, otherwise print "Try again".',"score = 72\n",B.a1R,B.a4m,B.a0Z,1,B.M,null,B.ZX)
B.a47=s(["Use a for loop","Use the range() function"],t.s)
B.a2f=s(["Use a for loop","Print 1 through 5"],t.s)
B.a1b=s(["for i in range(1, 6):","range(1, 6) gives 1,2,3,4,5"],t.s)
B.ZO=new A.cy('Copying the same line five times works, until you need it a hundred times. A loop is how you say "do this again" without writing it again.',null)
B.ZR=new A.cy("`for` takes each item in a list, one at a time, and runs the indented lines for that item. `range(1, 6)` makes the list `1, 2, 3, 4, 5` \u2014 it stops *before* the last number, which catches everyone.","for i in range(1, 6):\n    print(i)")
B.Zy=new A.cy("The `i` is just a name you choose. It starts as `1`, the loop runs, then it becomes `2`, runs again, and so on. On the last round it's `5`, and then the loop is done.",null)
B.Zr=new A.cy("The indented lines are the loop's body \u2014 they run once per number. Put them at the wrong indent and Python will tell you, loudly.",null)
B.a_s=s([B.ZO,B.ZR,B.Zy,B.Zr],t.xu)
B.ZY=new A.kj(B.a_s)
B.Uk=new A.dm("ex-loops","lesson-3","Loops","Print the numbers 1 to 5, one per line, using a loop.","# Use a for loop\n",B.a47,B.a2f,B.a1b,1,B.M,null,B.ZY)
B.a1S=s(["Define functions with def","Return values"],t.s)
B.a3B=s(["Define square(n)","Return n * n"],t.s)
B.a3c=s(["return n * n"],t.s)
B.ZC=new A.cy("A function is a recipe you name. You write it once, then call it by name as often as you like \u2014 no rewriting the steps each time.",null)
B.Zw=new A.cy("Start with `def`, then the name, then brackets. `n` is called a parameter: it's the ingredient the function needs. And the colon-and-indent rule applies exactly as it does with `if`.","def square(n):\n    return n * n")
B.ZH=new A.cy("Defining a function doesn't run it. Python just reads the recipe and remembers it. It only runs when you *call* it \u2014 brackets included.","def square(n):\n    return n * n\n\nprint(square(4))")
B.ZD=new A.cy("`return` is what sends the answer back out. Everything after it belongs to the function and stops there. Without `return`, a function gives back nothing at all.",null)
